---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **지렛대가 정말 커졌나.** (`/leverage`)
 *
 * ── 왜 이 지면인가 ─────────────────────────────────────────────
 *   업계가 지금 하는 이야기는 「제작비가 27배 올랐다 → 감당할 곳이 몇 안 된다 →
 *   넷플릭스 지렛대가 커졌다」다. ⛔ 제작비는 **우리에게 없다.**
 *   ⭐ 그러나 그 주장의 **결과**는 우리 것으로 잰다 — 차트가 소수에게 몰려 갔나.
 *   그리고 답은 **아니오**다. 몰림은 크지만 **늘지 않았고 오히려 줄었다.**
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ **남의 수와 우리 수를 한 표에 안 섞는다.** 제작비는 인용 상자 안에만 둔다.
 * ⛔ **회사를 줄세우지 않는다.** 「윗 세 곳」은 셈이지 이름표가 아니다 — 이름을 안 적는다.
 * ⛔ **못 가른 것을 발견처럼 안 적는다.** 방송 하락은 교란을 못 죽였다. 그렇게 적는다.
 * ⛔ 덜 찬 해(2021·2026)를 추세에 안 섞는다. 화면에 「덜 찬 해」라고 적는다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않는다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 `wikitip-leverage.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import d from '../../data/wikitip-leverage.json';

/*
 * ⛔ 온전한 해를 **지면에서 다시 고르지 않는다.** 수집기가 이미 골라 놨다.
 *   같은 셈을 두 곳에서 하면 언젠가 둘이 어긋나고, 어긋난 쪽이 화면에 나간다.
 */
const 첫 = d.byYear.find((y) => y.year === d.firstFullYear);
const 끝 = d.byYear.find((y) => y.year === d.lastFullYear);
const 꽉찬최대 = Math.max(...d.byYear.map((y) => y.wellRecordedTitles));

const TITLE = 'Did the charts concentrate? Korean series by company, year by year';
const DESC =
  `Production costs are reported to have risen 27-fold in under a decade, and the argument built on that `
  + `is that power concentrated. Across ${d.firstFullYear}–${d.lastFullYear} the three largest `
  + `companies' share of charting Korean series went from ${d.topThreeFirst}× an even split to `
  + `${d.topThreeLast}× — down, not up. Measured from ${d.rowsRead.toLocaleString('en-US')} chart rows.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://www.wikidata.org'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/leverage">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    {d.question}
  </p>

  <section class="warn-box">
    <h2>A figure that is not ours</h2>
    <p class="warn">
      <b>{d.costClaim.whatIsClaimed}</b>
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <b>Whose number.</b> {d.costClaim.whoseNumber} {d.costClaim.whyItIsHere} Every other figure on
      this page is counted from Netflix's published charts, and none of them is a cost.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How few companies cover half the year</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Year</th><th>Titles charting</th><th>Companies</th>
            <th>Half covered by</th><th>If shared equally</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.byYear.map((y) => (
            <tr class={y.weeks < 40 ? 'part' : undefined}>
              <td class="nm">{y.year}{y.weeks < 40 && <span class="fine"> — part year, {y.weeks} weeks</span>}</td>
              <td>{y.titlesWithFirm}</td>
              <td>{y.firms}</td>
              <td class="tot">{y.halfTakesFirms}</td>
              <td>{y.halfTakesIfEven}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <b>A title counts once</b> however many companies are credited on it, so covering "half" cannot
      be reached by adding the same title twice. {d.unit}
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <b>{첫.halfTakesFirms} companies covering half of {첫.titlesWithFirm} titles</b> is a great deal
      of concentration — an even spread would need {첫.halfTakesIfEven}. But it is the same figure at
      the start and the end of the period, and the column can only move a whole company at a time.
    </p>
    <p class="note fine">
      Read as a percentage that column gives {d.concentrationFirst}% in {d.firstFullYear} against{' '}
      {d.concentrationLast}% in {d.lastFullYear}, a change of {d.concentrationChange} points. We do
      not lead with that figure, because a measure that moves in whole companies cannot support a
      decimal — it is here so you can see it is not being hidden, not because it means anything.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The same question, with a measure that moves</h2>
    <p class="body">
      {d.whyTwoConcentrationMeasures}
    </p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Year</th><th>Top three cover</th><th>Even split would give</th><th>Ratio</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {d.byYear.map((y) => (
            <tr class={y.weeks < 40 ? 'part' : undefined}>
              <td class="nm">{y.year}{y.weeks < 40 && <span class="fine"> — part year</span>}</td>
              <td>{y.topThreePc}%</td>
              <td>{y.topThreeIfEven}%</td>
              <td class="tot">{y.topThreeRatio}×</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="q">
      {d.firstFullYear}: <b>{d.topThreeFirst}×</b> · {d.lastFullYear}: <b>{d.topThreeLast}×</b> —
      a change of <b>{d.topThreeChange}</b>. Down, not up.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <b>Concentration is high and did not increase.</b> Those are two different statements and both
      are on this table. The lowest the multiple ever falls is <b>{d.topThreeMin}×</b>, in{' '}
      {d.topThreeMinYear} — so the three largest companies cover several times an even share in every
      year on this table, including the part years. Across the full years, that multiple fell.
    </p>
    <p class="note fine">
      "The three largest" is a count, not a list. We do not name them, because which three they are
      changes between years and a name here would read as a ranking.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="warn-box">
    <h2>One thing we measured and are not reporting</h2>
    <p class="body">
      The share of charting titles that passed through a broadcaster falls from{' '}
      <b>{d.broadcastFirst}%</b> in {d.firstFullYear} to <b>{d.broadcastLast}%</b> in{' '}
      {d.lastFullYear}, a change of <b>{d.broadcastChange} points</b> — the shape you would expect if
      commissioning were moving away from Korean networks. It is the most interesting number we
      found, and it is not a finding.
    </p>
    <p class="warn">
      Recorded credits per title fall across the same years, from {d.rolesPerTitleFirst} to{' '}
      {d.rolesPerTitleLast}. A title that never had a broadcaster and a title whose broadcaster
      nobody has recorded yet look identical in this data.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <b>{d.confoundTestRan ? 'The control ran.' : 'The control does not have the strength to settle it.'}</b>
      {' '}{d.whyWellRecordedColumn}
    </p>
    {d.confoundTestRan ? (
      <p class="note">
        Years with enough well-recorded titles to run it: <b>{d.confoundTestUsableYears.join(', ')}</b>.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p class="note">
        <b>We require {d.confoundTestMinTitles} such titles in a year before we will read anything
        from it</b>, so that one title cannot move the figure by more than the decline being tested.
        No year reaches it — the best is {꽉찬최대}. So: <em>{d.broadcastVerdict}</em>
      </p>
    )}
    <p class="note fine">
      <b>We have been caught by this exact thing before.</b> A clean 69% decline in new K-pop group
      debuts turned out to be the speed at which Wikidata records new groups — the same decline
      appeared for American, Japanese and British groups.{' '}
      <a href="/article/the-decline-that-was-not-there">That one we could prove, and published</a>.
      This one we cannot prove either way, which is the only difference between them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="src">
    <h2>How this was counted, and what it cannot say</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Source</b> — {d.source}</li>
      <li><b>Rows read</b> — {d.rowsRead.toLocaleString('en-US')} weekly country chart rows,
        {' '}{d.yearsMeasured} years.</li>
      <li><b>Part years</b> — {d.partialYears.join(' and ')} are shown but excluded from every
        comparison, because a part year is not comparable with a full one. The trend is read from{' '}
        {d.firstFullYear} to {d.lastFullYear}.</li>
      <li><b>Why every year has an "if shared equally" column</b> — {d.whyEvenComparison}</li>
      <li><b>Russia is excluded</b> throughout this site, because Netflix withdrew from it and the
        remaining weeks are not comparable with the others.</li>
      <li><b>Wikidata is incomplete</b> — a credit records that a company is attached, not how much of
        the work it held, and a company Wikidata does not record is absent rather than small.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="warn">
      {d.cannotAnswer}
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="/who-makes-it">How many companies fill half the shelf</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/clumping">Why 7.7% is not one place a week</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/rank-tells">What a chart position does not say</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/for-industry">What we can hand over</a> · <a href="/about">How we count</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/leverage" />
</WikiTip>

<style>
  tr.part td { opacity: 0.62; }
  .warn-box { border-left: 3px solid #b45309; padding-left: 1rem; }
</style>
